{"task_id": "Mbpp/131", "solution": "def reverse_vowels(input: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Reverses only the vowels of a given string, preserving non-vowel positions and casing.\"\"\"\n\n    VOWELS = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u', 'A', 'E', 'I', 'O', 'U'}\n\n    def extractVowels(s: str) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:\n        \"\"\"Extracts vowels and their original indices from a string.\"\"\"\n        vowel_positions = []\n        for i, char in enumerate(s):\n            if char in VOWELS:\n                vowel_positions.append((i, char))\n        return vowel_positions\n\n    def reverseVowels(vowels_list: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n        \"\"\"Returns a reversed copy of the list of vowel characters.\"\"\"\n        return vowels_list[::-1]\n\n    def reintegrate(original_string: str, reversedVowels: list[str]) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Replaces original vowel positions in a string with the reversed vowels.\"\"\"\n        string_chars = list(original_string)\n        \n        # We need the vowel positions again to know where to insert the reversed vowels.\n        # This is slightly redundant with extractVowels, but ensures correct placement.\n        # A more optimized approach might pass vowel_positions directly or use a single pass.\n        # However, following the detailed design, extractVowels returns (index, char),\n        # then we get just chars, then reverse chars, then reintegrate needs to know indices again.\n        # For simplicity and adherence to the separate function breakdown in design,\n        # re-extracting the indices (or iterating through the previously extracted positions)\n        # seems to align. Let's iterate through the already computed vowel_positions.\n\n        vowel_indices_only = [idx for idx, _ in extractVowels(original_string)]\n        \n        for i, char_to_place in zip(vowel_indices_only, reversedVowels):\n            string_chars[i] = char_to_place\n            \n        return \"\".join(string_chars)\n\n    vowel_positions_and_chars = extractVowels(input)\n\n    if len(vowel_positions_and_chars) <= 1:\n        return input\n\n    vowels_only_chars = [char for _, char in vowel_positions_and_chars]\n    reversed_vowels_chars = reverseVowels(vowels_only_chars)\n\n    return reintegrate(input, reversed_vowels_chars)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/138", "solution": "class Number:\n    \"\"\"Represents an input integer.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"Wraps the boolean representability outcome.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, isRepresentable: bool):\n        self.isRepresentable = isRepresentable\nclass RepresentationChecker:\n    \"\"\"Stateless service to validate input and determine representability.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _validate_input(n):\n        \"\"\"Validates that n is an integer, raising TypeError if not.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n        return Number(n)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_Sum_Of_Powers_Of_Two(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if n can be represented as a sum of non-zero powers of 2.\"\"\"\n        # Handles n <= 0 and checks for evenness based on design rules.\n        if n <= 0:\n            return Result(False).isRepresentable\n\n        # For positive n, representable if even (least significant bit is 0).\n        is_even = (n & 1) == 0\n        return Result(is_even).isRepresentable\ndef is_Sum_Of_Powers_Of_Two(n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number can be represented as a sum of non-zero powers of 2.\"\"\"\n    RepresentationChecker._validate_input(n)\n    return RepresentationChecker.is_Sum_Of_Powers_Of_Two(n)"}
