{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(input_list: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a pair of numbers with the highest product from a given array of integers.\n    The pair is returned as an ordered tuple (a, b) where a <= b.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(input_list) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"input_list must contain at least two integers\")\n\n    max_product_val = -float('inf')\n    best_pair: Tuple[int, int] = (0, 0) # Initialize with arbitrary values, will be overwritten\n\n    n = len(input_list)\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n): # Ensures distinct indices and avoids duplicate symmetric pairs\n            val1 = input_list[i]\n            val2 = input_list[j]\n            current_product = val1 * val2\n\n            if current_product > max_product_val:\n                max_product_val = current_product\n                # Ensure the pair is stored such that a <= b\n                if val1 <= val2:\n                    best_pair = (val1, val2)\n                else:\n                    best_pair = (val2, val1)\n    return best_pair"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/420", "solution": "def cube_Sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the cube sum of the first n even natural numbers.\n    The sum is for (2k)^3 for k from 1 to n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    # The sum of cubes of first n natural numbers is (n * (n + 1) / 2)^2\n    # So, sum of (2k)^3 for k=1 to n is 8 * (sum of k^3 for k=1 to n)\n    # This simplifies to 8 * (n * (n + 1) // 2) ** 2\n    sum_of_natural_cubes = (n * (n + 1) // 2) ** 2\n    result = 8 * sum_of_natural_cubes\n    return result"}
